\description{
\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#experimental}{\figure{lifecycle-experimental.svg}{options: alt='[Experimental]'}}}{\strong{[Experimental]}}

Finds the compositions with the best predicted outcome for one \code{\link[=comp_iv]{comp_iv()}}
fit, or one fit per outcome, among candidates on a grid in closure units (by
default every 10 minutes of a 1,440-minute day) inside a feasible region of
the sample's compositions. Each outcome's zone holds the candidates whose
prediction ranks in its best \code{1 - zone} share or meets a \code{threshold}, and
the closed geometric mean of the zones' intersection is the optimal
composition. The search method, its arguments and its defaults may change
in a later release.
}
\details{
Every prediction comes from \code{emmeans}, on the reference grid
\code{\link[=predict_realloc]{predict_realloc()}} uses. With \code{type = "relative"} (default), a candidate's
value is its contrast with \code{from} (by default the reference composition) on
the fit's effect scale, as for a \code{\link[=predict_realloc]{predict_realloc()}} reallocation, so every
engine of \code{\link[=comp_iv]{comp_iv()}} can be searched. With \code{type = "absolute"}, the value is
the predicted response.

The search runs in this order.
\enumerate{
\item Region. The feasible region is estimated from the sample's
compositions, per level of \code{by}. The default \code{"box"} limits every part,
on the closure scale, to its mean \eqn{\pm}{+/-} \code{sd} standard
deviations, 3 by default (Dumuid et al. 2021). With \code{coverage}, the
limits are a pair of the part's quantiles instead (Brakenridge et al.
2024 use the 1st and 99th percentiles). No limit extends beyond the
part's observed range. The \code{"ellipsoid"} region holds the compositions
whose squared Mahalanobis distance from the mean of the pivot isometric
log-ratio (ILR) coordinates is at most the \code{coverage} quantile of the
chi-square distribution on \eqn{D - 1} degrees of freedom (Mellow et al.
2026). That distance is
\eqn{(z - \bar z)^\top S^{-1} (z - \bar z)}{(z - zbar)' S^-1 (z - zbar)},
with \eqn{S} the sample covariance. \code{"observed"} takes the sample's own
compositions as the candidates (Lund Rasmussen et al. 2023), and a data
frame takes the compositions it holds.
\item Candidates. For \code{"box"} and \code{"ellipsoid"}, the candidates are the
compositions inside the region whose first \code{D - 1} parts are positive
multiples of \code{spacing}, the last part being the remainder of the closure
declared in \code{\link[=declare_composition]{declare_composition()}}. The default \code{spacing} is 1/144 of
that closure, the 10-minute grid of Dumuid et al. (2021) on a
1,440-minute day; Mellow et al. (2026) use 5 minutes. The search stops
with an error when more than 1,000,000 candidates lie inside the region,
or when the grid holds more than 5,000,000 compositions before the
region is applied.
\item Prediction. Each outcome is predicted at every candidate on the fit's
link scale. For \code{type = "relative"}, each prediction is contrasted there
with the prediction at \code{from}. As in \code{\link[=predict_realloc]{predict_realloc()}}, factor
covariates that do not interact with the composition are averaged with
equal weight, and numeric ones are held at their mean. Each \code{emmeans}
reference grid holds many candidates, so a search over tens of thousands
of them takes a few seconds.
\item Zone. A candidate's rank is the share of candidates whose link-scale
prediction is no better than its own (ties averaged), so it is the same
for both values of \code{type}. An outcome's zone holds the candidates whose
rank is at least \code{zone}. For an outcome named in \code{threshold}, a candidate
is in the zone when its value on the \code{type} scale (the effect versus
\code{from}, or the predicted response) meets the cut-off or lies in the band
(Vindiš et al. 2025). In this package, the rule "healthy below the 85th
percentile" of Lund Rasmussen et al. (2023) is
\verb{direction = "min", zone = 0.15}, which keeps 85\% of the candidates.
\item Intersection. The overall zone holds the candidates in every outcome's
zone (Dumuid et al. 2021; Duncan et al. 2024). When the zones do not
overlap, \code{q_overlap} gives the largest \code{zone} at which they would share
a candidate, and the intersection stays empty unless \code{widen = TRUE}.
Widening lowers \code{zone} for every outcome it rules, together, in steps of
0.05 (\code{zone - 0.05}, \code{zone - 0.10}, ...) until the zones share a
candidate, as in Dumuid et al. (2021) and Brakenridge et al. (2024). It
stops at the first such step and never goes below 0.5, the best 50\% of
each outcome. A \code{threshold} is never changed. The result is that of a
search at the level reached, which \code{zone_used} reports. A widened zone
may not improve every outcome, and each outcome's gain and its interval
show whether it does.
\item Centre. Each zone is summarized by its closed geometric mean (the
inverse ILR of its mean coordinates), its per-part range and its
best-ranked candidate.
\item Gain. Each outcome is predicted at the overall zone centre (at its own
centre when the zones do not overlap) and contrasted with \code{from}, as one
\code{\link[=predict_realloc]{predict_realloc()}} contrast. For \code{type = "relative"}, this gain is also
the value reported at the centre.
}

A predicted outcome linear in the ILR coordinates has no interior optimum,
and its zone lies on the edge of the region. A fit with no curvature term
(squares, products, \code{poly()}, \code{ns()}, \code{bs()}) is still searched, with one
message stating that the fit is linear in the composition. The zone of a
curved fit also lies on the boundary when its stationary point is outside
the region.

A zone's \code{on_boundary} value is the share of its candidates in the outer
shell of the region. On the ellipsoid, that shell holds the candidates whose
squared Mahalanobis distance is above 0.9 of the limit; on the box, those
with a part within one \code{spacing} of a limit.

The confidence intervals of the prediction at the centre and of the gain
are conditional on the centre, and the location of the zone itself has no
interval.

The box, the ellipsoid and the grid do not depend on the order of the parts
when \code{spacing} divides the total, as the default does.

A covariate that interacts with the composition must be pinned with \code{at} or
split with \code{by}; otherwise the call aborts and names it. A factor moderator
is split by default, as in \code{\link[=predict_realloc]{predict_realloc()}}. With \code{by}, each level has
its own region, estimated from its own rows, and its own zone. On a fit
built with \code{include_total = TRUE}, every prediction holds \code{t_total} at the
fit's \code{total_ref}.

\code{type = "absolute"} is refused for the engines without an absolute
prediction (\code{"cox"}, \code{"coxme"}, \code{"clm"}, \code{"clmm"} and \code{"mblogit"}; see
\code{\link[=comp_iv]{comp_iv()}}). A multinomial (\code{"mblogit"}) fit is searched on the log-odds of
the outcome level named by \code{category} against the reference level, so each
\code{category} has its own ranking and zone.
}
\section{Functions}{
\itemize{
\item \code{print(optimal_comp)}: Prints a text report of the search, zone by
zone. Under the region it gives each part's limits (the box's, or the
candidates' range for another region), per level of \code{by}, and the number
of box limits cut to the observed range when there are any. For a level
whose zones were widened, a line above its zones gives the share reached.
For \code{type = "absolute"}, the report adds the predicted outcome at each
zone centre.

\item \code{plot(optimal_comp)}: Ternary panels (\code{choose(D, 3)}) of one
outcome's surface on the search's \code{type} scale (the effect versus \code{from},
or the predicted response), drawn for every engine. An ordinal fit's
surface is its cumulative odds ratio versus \code{from}, and a multinomial
fit's is the relative risk ratio of the searched \code{category}. Each panel is
a slice through the zone centre (the overall one, or the outcome's own
when the zones do not overlap). The surface is drawn as \code{\link[=plot.comp_iv]{plot.comp_iv()}}
draws one, on the cells inside the search's region. For the box and the
ellipsoid these are the cells the region itself holds (on the parameters
of the \code{level} drawn); otherwise they are the cells within the
candidates' per-part range. The surface holds the search's \code{at} values
and, for a search with \code{by}, the \code{level} drawn. The convex hull of the
computed cells in the outcome's zone is outlined in red, and the centre is
marked. A panel with fewer than three computed cells in the zone has no
outline, and a higher \code{resolution} computes more cells. A panel carries no
caption: the zone centre, the held covariates and the region's limits are
in \code{\link[=print]{print()}}. Returns the plot object (one plot, or a list named by
triplet), drawn as a side effect. An argument the method does not take is
an error.

}}
\examples{
\donttest{
cd <- declare_composition(timeuse, c("MVPA", "LPA", "SB", "SPT"),
                          closure = 1440, units = "min/day")
quad <- comp_iv(cd, formula = zbmi ~ ilr()^2 + I(ilr()^2) + sex,
                model = "lm")
# The per-part box of the mean +/- 3 standard deviations, on a 10-minute grid
opt <- optimal_composition(quad, direction = "min")
opt

# Two outcomes, their zones intersected
risk <- comp_iv(cd, "obesity", model = "glm", family = "binomial")
optimal_composition(list(zbmi = quad, obesity = risk), direction = "min")

# The chi-square ellipsoid at the 80\% level
optimal_composition(quad, direction = "min", region = "ellipsoid")

# A box from the 1st to the 99th percentile of each part, on a 20-minute grid
optimal_composition(quad, direction = "min", coverage = c(0.01, 0.99),
                    spacing = 20)

# A Cox fit, searched for the lowest hazard ratio versus the reference day
cox <- comp_iv(cd, formula = survival::Surv(time, event) ~ ilr()^2 +
                 I(ilr()^2) + sex, model = "cox")
optimal_composition(list(onset = cox), direction = "min")
}

}
